// SERVER WORKS -- the crowd bots.
//
// GENERATED by dev/gen_characterset.py. Do not hand-edit: re-run it instead,
// and re-run it after a game update in case vanilla's own mechanics change.
//
// One entry per LOOK, which is how vanilla gets ten different-looking mechanics
// (Survival/Character/CharacterSets/npc_mechanics.json) and is NOT how this mod
// did it at first. The bots used to be a single entry dressed at runtime by
// character:overrideRenderableList with a list built per bot. MEASURED cost of
// that, from /bench:
//
//     0 bots   60.0 fps      10 bots  15.0 fps      20 bots  8 fps
//
// against 30 fps for NINETY-FIVE bots in an earlier run where the appearance
// code was throwing and every bot silently shared the fallback outfit. A fixed
// renderable list per entry is loaded once and shared by every character that
// uses it; a bespoke list per character is twenty sets of meshes the renderer
// cannot batch.
//
// vanilla calls overrideRenderableList in exactly one script in the whole game,
// for one quest NPC. That was the tell, and it was there all along.
//
// The renderables below are copied verbatim from characters that exist in the
// game -- vanilla's ten NPC mechanics, plus the classic mechanic that
// Data/Character/CharacterSets/default.json dresses the PLAYER in. Nothing here
// is an asset a player could not see in game, which was the other half of the
// problem: the hand-built lists pulled in cosmetics that are on disk and not
// obtainable.
//
// The capsule, hit hulls, ragdoll and tumble hull are vanilla's own for a human.
// The MOVEMENT block is the PLAYER's, out of default.json, because a stand-in
// for a player should move at a player's speed -- vanilla's NPC mechanics sprint
// at 8.0, which is twice as fast as anyone.
